  <title>Circles and ellipses</title>

  <p><gui>+ Circle</gui> adds a circle (<code>^GC</code> in ZPL) and
  <gui>+ Ellipse</gui> an ellipse (<code>^GE</code>). They are different
  elements: a circle can never be stretched into an oval.</p>

  <terms>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Diameter</gui></title>
      <p>A circle's size in dots, 3 to 4095. Its box is a square exactly the
      diameter wide. A circle always stays round: a side handle sets the
      diameter from that side, and a corner handle takes the smaller of the
      two sides you dragged, so the circle stays inside the box.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Width</gui> and <gui>Height</gui></title>
      <p>An ellipse's size in dots, 3 to 4095 each, set freely. Its handles
      change the box exactly as you drag it.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Thickness</gui></title>
      <p>The ring, drawn inward from the outline, so the box stays the
      outside. At the radius (half the shorter side, for an ellipse) the
      shape is solid black.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title><gui>Colour</gui> and <gui>Reverse</gui></title>
      <p>As for a <link xref="frame">frame</link>: white prints only over
      something black, and <gui>Reverse</gui> turns what is beneath the ring
      white.</p>
    </item>
  </terms>

  <section id="centre">
    <title>Put a circle's centre on a point</title>
    <p><gui>X</gui> and <gui>Y</gui> are the top left corner of the circle's
    box, not its centre. To centre a circle on a point, subtract half the
    diameter from the point: a circle 150 dots across, centred on 400, 300,
    goes at <gui>X</gui> 325, <gui>Y</gui> 225. Or line it up with something
    else using <guiseq><gui>Edit</gui><gui>Align</gui></guiseq>.</p>
  </section>

  <note>
    <p>The canvas draws an ellipse's ring the thickness you set at the ends
    of both its axes, so on a long, narrow ellipse it is thinner where the
    curve turns. How a printer draws that ring has not yet been checked
    against one. The difference would be nothing on a round ellipse and
    under a dot on a moderately long one, growing the longer and narrower
    the ellipse is.</p>
  </note>
</page>
